  "pli-tv-kd17:3.13.19": "",
  "pli-tv-kd17:3.13.20": "",
  "pli-tv-kd17:3.13.21": "See <a href='https://suttacentral.net/pli-tv-bu-vb-pc32/en/brahmali#8.15'>Bu Pc 32:8.15</a>. ",
  "pli-tv-kd17:3.14.1": "",
  "pli-tv-kd17:3.14.2": "“Break its authority” renders <i lang='pi' translate='no'>cakkabheda</i>. Sp 1.410: <i lang='pi' translate='no'>Cakkabhedāyāti āṇābhedāya</i>, “<i lang='pi' translate='no'>Cakkabheda</i>: by breaking the authority.” Vjb 4.343: <i lang='pi' translate='no'>Cakkabhedanti sāsanabhedaṁ</i>, “<i lang='pi' translate='no'>Cakkabheda</i>: a break in the instruction.” The break in authority is presumably both from the Buddha and the Sangha. Although the Buddha was the only authority in laying down rules, the Sangha was autonomous in its decision making. For practical purposes, it was the Sangha that Devadatta was breaking with. ",
